--TEST--
a relative path that reaches a `Core` door at run time throws a `RuntimeError` naming the path and both ways to make it absolute, before any grant is asked, and `Core\Path::fromCwd` or `Core\Path::join` makes it one the door accepts — `rule:programs/relative-paths-resolve-from-their-file`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:programs/file-paths-a-relative-path-starts-at-the-folder-of-its-file
// The literal is assigned to a variable, so it is not written at a path
// parameter, and the compiler leaves it as it is.
string $name = 'data/note.txt';
try {
    echo Core\IO::read($name);
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}

// The same refusal at a door that writes. A path joined with `.` from a
// variable is built while the program runs.
string $dir = 'data';
try {
    Core\IO::write($dir . '/copy.txt', 'x');
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}

// `fromCwd` joins it to the folder the program was started from.
echo Core\IO::read(Core\Path::fromCwd($name));

// `join` builds it from a folder the program already holds as an absolute path.
string $folder = Core\Path::fromCwd('data');
echo Core\IO::read(Core\Path::join($folder, 'note.txt'));
--FILE data/note.txt--
read
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["./data"]
--EXPECT--
Core\IO::read needs an absolute path, and `data/note.txt` is relative
help: build the path with `Core\Path::join` from a folder you know, or with `Core\Path::fromCwd` for a path typed on the command line
Core\IO::write needs an absolute path, and `data/copy.txt` is relative
help: build the path with `Core\Path::join` from a folder you know, or with `Core\Path::fromCwd` for a path typed on the command line
read
read
